  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 100 PM MDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nOn Monday, upper trough ejects eastward across CO, with precip\ntapering off over the southwest mountains through the day as\nflow becomes more w-nw, while showers linger elsewhere over the\nremaining mountains and valleys. Strong cold front will swing \nthrough the plains early Mon as surface/mid level low moves \ninto KS, with strong north winds and a period of showers and \nthunderstorms likely along and east of I-25 as vertical motion \nincreases under the deformation band. Will have to watch for a \nburst of heavier precip (rain and snow) along the east \nslopes/Palmer Divide/Raton Mesa Mon afternoon/evening, especially\nif surface winds can take on a a slightly more nely component. \nWind gusts could get close to 60 mph on the plains as low wraps\nup, and will have to monitor areas near the KS border for a \npotential wind highlight if model trends continue. Precip then \nends Mon evening, with a dry day on Tue as upper ridge briefly \nreturns to the area. \n\nStill some uncertainty concerning the mid/late week forecast, as\nmodel solutions now show weak energy sliding through the Rockies\nWed/Thu, generating a few showers/mtn snow showers across the\nregion through the period. Not expecting heavy precip as system\nwill be rather moisture-starved, though some light snow\naccumulations over the higher mountains look possible. Solutions\nregarding the position and timing of the next major upper \ntrough for late week into the weekend still vary widely, though \nan eastward shift in the trough position has been noted in the\noverall model/ensemble data, which could limit impacts to srn\nCO.",
